==Japanese==

===Pronunciation===
Native speakers usually say it as:
	•	ちょうじげん → [chóò jígèn] (high-low-low-low)
So the “chō” is a bit higher in pitch than “jigen”.

===Adjective===

# {{lb|en|science fiction}} 「超次元」 (chō jigen) literally means “hyperdimensional” or “super-dimensional” in Japanese, and it is commonly used as a synonym for “extradimensional” in English, especially in the context of sci-fi and fantasy worlds.

In simple terms, “hyperdimensional” and “super-dimensional” are words used to describe things that exist in more dimensions than our ordinary 3D space (length, width, height) — and often more than the usual 4D (3 space + 1 time) of physics.

When people say hyperdimensional or super-dimensional, they usually mean 5 dimensions or higher — so they’re talking about extra spatial directions or extra properties that we cannot directly see.